00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:35.060
sector rotation patterns and is adjusting entry filters accordingly. Imagine you bought a broad market

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:43.400
ETF last week when the NASDAQ dipped 1.5%. Your order filled at the closing NAV. But what actually happened

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:49.380
between your click and that price was a silent auction among authorized participants who arbitrage

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:55.960
any premium or discount to the underlying basket in real time. The mechanism keeps tracking error

00:07:55.960 --> 00:08:04.960
under 0.05% for most S&amp;P 500 ETFs because creation and redemption units of 50,000 shares force

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:12.080
market makers to keep the ETF price within a few basis points of fair value. What most retail investors

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:19.180
do not realize is that this same mechanism is why active mutual funds with 2% or higher MERs

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:25.640
have delivered negative alpha versus the index in 85% of rolling 10 year periods after fees.

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:31.800
The professionals always check the ETF's premium and discount history on the exchange website

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:39.340
before allocating because even a 0.3% persistent premium compounds into meaningful drag over a decade.

00:08:40.040 --> 00:08:45.960
The biggest mistake with index investing is assuming set and forget means ignoring costs entirely.

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:53.760
Instead, always compare the ETF's expense ratio and bid-ask spread against the mutual fund alternative

00:08:53.760 --> 00:09:00.200
